WEBVTT

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.720
'I'm standing on the rim 'of one of the most active volcanoes on the planet.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:28.560
'It's so powerful, it once blew the top off this entire island.'

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:33.880
This is Marum, with its truly breathtaking lava lake.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:44.320
But to understand how this volcano is behaving, we need to get off the rim of this crater and descend towards that fiery inferno.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:59.200
'I've travelled over 10,000 miles to join a team of scientists 'as they attempt to get under the skin of this volcano.'

00:00:58.280 --> 00:00:59.440
I can feel the heat now.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:03.240
'It's a challenge fraught with risk.'

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.640
Ooo!

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:04.200
Sorry!

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:04.440
Oh, Tim, sorry!

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:05.800
It's OK, no problem.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.120
'But it could give us an insight into its inner workings 'never achieved before.'

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.040
That's enough to kill you pretty quickly, actually.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:23.920
It can pump out more sulphur dioxide than anywhere else on earth in a toxic plume of gas that engulfs the island and its people.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.480
So I'll also be finding out how it affects the lives of the islanders.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.040
What's so funny exactly?

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:40.480
Is this volcano building towards another major eruption?

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:41.280
Oh, my God, it's really warm!

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:41.600
Argh!

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:43.960
Whoa!

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:16.160
I'm flying above the island of Ambrym in the Vanuatu archipelago.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:23.840
This island is home to one of the most dynamic and exciting volcanoes in the world.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:26.280
It's called Marum.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.720
And we're here to try and understand how this volcano works.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.240
What its personality is.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:38.360
Ambrym is one of the islands in the Vanuatu chain that sits on the edge of the Pacific Ring of Fire.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:43.480
Its volcano contains one of just five lava lakes in the world.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:50.680
A permanently-bubbling cauldron of molten rock that makes it unique.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:03:01.200
I've joined Professor Shane Cronin, who has been monitoring this volcano for a decade.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.400
I'd say it's dark and brooding.

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:08.120
You've got this black ash, the lava, the shapes.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.760
He's here to find out if its behaviour is shifting.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:19.120
It brings me back time and time again to understand its moods, its changes, its effects on people.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:19.880
And what makes this volcano particularly unusual and special?

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.720
One of the things about it that's so unusual is it's always erupting.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:27.280
It's not always throwing material in the air, but it's almost always erupting.

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:29.480
There's almost always something going on.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.480
Magma is always there.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.800
It's alive, it's pulsing.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:37.200
And to understand the breadth of those changes, we do need to get a feel for that personality.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:45.760
When its moods shift, what might indicate a mood shift and what might indicate a hazard for the people living on the island.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:49.240
'This whole island is a volcano.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:57.520
'Its landscape scarred by the string of violent eruptions 'which have frequently forced entire villages to be evacuated.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:08.360
'So, what's it like for the 7,000 people 'who live their lives on the slopes of an active volcano?'

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:14.400
Hi.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:14.760
I'm Kate.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.560
Hello, Kate.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.680
Welcome to Ambrym.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.440
Thank you.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.839
Oh, how beautiful!

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.880
They smell lovely.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.280
It's a pleasure.

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:28.960
Look at this fantastic village!

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:35.440
'As soon as we arrive, we're greeted Vanuatu-style.'

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:39.960
Is this a normal occurrence when you come to an island like this?

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:47.880
Well, on an island like this, when a plane comes, or when a cargo boat comes in, you know, it's a big occasion.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.360
They look cute, don't they?

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:52.840
They look amazing.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:53.360
Hello!

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.400
Say hello.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:54.760
Hello!

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.640
How are you?

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.160
Hello?

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:00.960
What's so funny exactly?

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:02.080
Hm?

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:05.280
It's very, very strange just dropping into a community like this.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:12.960
You come in across this thick, dense vegetation covering this incredibly rugged landscape.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.400
And that's all because of the volcano that totally dominates this island.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:28.800
But then you get to a place like this and it feels so benign and friendly and you can't believe that just up there is a great boiling pit of lava.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:29.960
And the impact on these villages will be enormous.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:33.520
It's like Christmas.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:38.040
Santa's sack!

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:45.640
'Ambrym is so remote that planning a trip to the heart of its volcano 'is a bit like a military operation.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:54.600
'Everything we need has to be flown in and carried with us 'on the eight-hour hike to the summit in a few days' time.'

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:54.880
You like to pack light.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:02.200
I thought you just needed one little hammer.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:08.640
'I'm joining Shane and geologist Chris Firth...'

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.520
It looks like it.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:13.480
I don't recognise any... '..as they attempt something they've never tried before.'

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:14.280
I've got somebody else's bags entirely!

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:22.400
'Abseiling right into this volcano 'to find out if it's becoming more threatening.

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:32.880
'But before we embark on our arduous journey to the top, 'we'll be spending a few days in the village of Ranvetlam 'so we can gather some crucial evidence 'about the forces at work deep below us.'

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:33.960
So this is where you'll be staying.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.880
It's beautiful.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:43.960
'Here, like everywhere on Vanuatu, pigs are part of village life.'

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:45.680
I love pigs.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:49.960
They roam around the village.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.520
Just completely free?

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:52.200
Yeah.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:53.040
But they belong to someone.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:53.440
OK.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.360
So you're saying, don't steal one.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:56.840
No.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:58.040
No.

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:13.720
The threat of an eruption is ever-present on Ambrym, so Chris and Shane want to gauge the level of risk Marum poses to the people living here.

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:28.880
Volcanoes are highly volatile and unpredictable, but Shane helped devise a scale of risk for Ambrym's volcano that's internationally recognised.

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:41.760
We're here to find out if Marum's hazard level needs to be increased and the islanders warned of any imminent danger.

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:59.000
So, Shane, can you give me a sort of geological lie of the land?

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.720
So we are located right up here, by Ranvetlam at the moment.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:06.440
So we're in that northern Ambrym and Marum is here.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:06.840
And we are north of the caldera.

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:14.800
So the central part of the island, this caldera system is where the bulk of the magma is right here.

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:23.040
This volcano, if not unique, certainly unusual is the fact that it has this open lava lake, doesn't it?

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:24.240
And not even one, but more than one.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:24.560
Which is certainly unique amongst volcanoes.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.320
This volcano is more or less in a constant state of eruption.

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:36.559
So when you look at the eruptive history of it and see a lava lake here, lava lake there, it comes and goes, the whole thing is always in a state of flux.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:45.200
Do you know yet whether it could then go into a more violent, eruptive stage than simply being a lava lake?

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:47.880
The best example we have of that is about 2005.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:56.120
In 2005, this was the biggest point source of sulphur dioxide on the planet.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.840
Wow!

00:08:55.960 --> 00:09:10.040
So that's the sort of thing we will be looking for in terms of trying to understand the state of the volcano is how much gas is coming out of it, how vigorous, or how active the lava lake happens to be and what other features and signs we see of different types of activity.

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:21.440
'In a couple of days, I'll be coming face to face with Marum, 'and I'm already feeling anxious.'

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:35.280
We've sent a team ahead to the summit.

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:41.520
They're battling the elements and setting up camp.

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:42.760
Sound of a good bolt is if it's singing to you.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:46.160
Hear that ding-ding?

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:47.000
Beautiful.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:55.040
A team of six specialists is making the crucial preparations for us to abseil into the volcano so we can collect the evidence we need to find out if it's changing.

00:09:54.160 --> 00:10:00.080
The weather up there is highly unpredictable.

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:06.840
And until they've finished building base camp, we can't join them.

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:19.240
So for the next few days, I'll be finding out more about the island and its volcano to help us when we get there.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:26.040
Of all the Vanuatu islands, Ambrym is the most notorious for black magic.

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:29.000
Marum is believed to hold the spirits of the islanders' ancestors.

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:33.480
And the complex systems of belief here are deeply connected to the power of the volcano.

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:39.760
So here, Kate, look, they are just weaving coconut leaves together to go on top of the housing.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:42.840
Ah!

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:43.320
And I love the fact they, um...

00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:52.480
'I've asked anthropologist Haidy Geismar, 'who has worked here over many years, 'to help us understand how the volcano shapes what people believe.'

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:55.520
The volcano is an intrinsic part of the environment.

00:10:55.320 --> 00:11:04.880
It affects the crops, it affects the soil, the fertility of the soil and the very ways in which people live.

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:13.800
Are there lots of stories, are there lots of legends, I don't know, songs, carvings, that reflect the volcano, or not so much?

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:14.000
Knowledge in Vanuatu is very placed in particular locations.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:18.880
And stories don't float around, they belong to places and people from particular places.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:28.720
We've heard one story which talks of a mysterious cave on the coast.

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:32.800
A place believed to have the power to kill.

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:37.960
We want to know if this power might be linked to the volcano.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:40.120
Morning.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:40.480
Morning.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:43.680
Chief Harry, Kate.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:44.160
Hello.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.720
Shane.

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:47.240
This is Chief Harry.

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:50.520
He's the chief of Ranvetlam.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:54.760
'Chief Harry has local knowledge that might be of help to us.'

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:57.560
You know when you want something where...

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:05.280
Only find them in the 18th, 19th century.

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:11.760
They saw that every time anything went inside, it died.

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:21.520
So it's a place that people have known about these powers for quite some time and sometimes in the past, they have used them malevolently.

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:28.160
If you take someone's leftover food and put it inside the cave, then that person would die.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:36.120
So Menuhin is a language word which means breathing.

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:36.560
It means you become short of breath.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:39.120
It pulls away your breath.

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:41.720
OK.

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:47.920
If people are short of breath, it sounds like carbon dioxide.

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:49.240
Right.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:53.440
I mean, a lot of people are killed by carbon dioxide in volcanic areas.

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:55.480
It's a silent killer.

00:12:54.520 --> 00:13:02.200
'Traditional beliefs in this village go hand in hand 'with a desire to better understand the volcano using science.

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:13.640
'And even though the cave is taboo, 'the importance of Shane's work here is well recognised.'

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:20.840
So I'm allowed to take my measuring instruments down.

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:21.120
OK.

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:24.400
Just don't take part of my breakfast down.

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:29.680
Otherwise I'm going to be suspecting your motives.

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:37.160
'Shane's convinced Harry is talking about a volcanic vent.

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:45.120
'A deep crack in the earth which is releasing 'potentially-deadly amounts of carbon dioxide.

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:54.720
'If he's right, it could be a clue to Marum's current behaviour.'

00:13:56.360 --> 00:14:00.000
Do you think this is it here, Shane, in front of us?

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:02.680
It looks like it.

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:04.800
Oh, horrible!

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:05.400
Oh!

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:05.760
Jeepers!

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.680
That's strong.

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:08.360
So clearly, there is something amiss here.

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:18.040
There are a lot of dead birds in the cave.

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:23.720
So that's drawing the gas in and over this tube.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:24.200
Yeah.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:28.760
And we see we've just gone down to the five, or just beyond the five, probably about 6%.

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:29.840
Yeah.

00:14:28.960 --> 00:14:33.640
Which I have to say doesn't sound that high, but what are sort of normal levels of carbon dioxide?

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:38.280
It's more than ten times normal levels.

00:14:37.400 --> 00:14:38.480
So tell us what that one says.

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:42.600
It looks much the same.

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:44.160
Maybe even a tiny bit higher.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:48.120
It's gone quite a bit more.

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:50.800
It's ten.

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:51.600
It's turned to ten.

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:52.160
That looks like ten.

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:53.120
Yeah.

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:53.400
Yeah.

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:53.960
Wow!

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:55.920
So I suppose because it's a heavier gas...

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:58.640
It's sunk down.

00:14:58.280 --> 00:14:59.880
Yeah.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:00.280
I'm going to have to move.

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:00.920
That's enough to kill you pretty quickly, actually.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:02.440
10%.

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:07.680
And could that be a precursor to a bigger eruptive event?

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:07.960
Yeah.

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:13.200
We would need to measure this constantly and know whether this gets more or less.

00:15:14.720 --> 00:15:24.560
But definitely, this would be a place where regular monitoring, if there was a big spike in the carbon dioxide output here, it would be worth definitely warning people.

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:31.480
Presumably, if that much carbon dioxide is being pumped out of here, standing around here for much longer is not a good idea.

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:35.400
No, I don't think it's a good idea for us to be overnighting here!

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:51.720
'The surprisingly high levels of carbon dioxide suggest 'that magma is rising from deep below, 'making the threat of a big eruption more likely.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:58.840
'It's enough to make me a bit nervous about what we might find 'when we reach the summit in a few days' time.

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:16.320
'Whilst Chris and Shane prepare their kit for the trek up...

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:21.280
'..I'm heading to an annual ritual 'that I've been told I just shouldn't miss.

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:32.920
'The rugged volcanic landscape and old lava flows 'separate the villages here.

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:38.280
'So even on this small island, four different languages are spoken.

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:44.960
'But shared systems of trade and barter bind everyone together.'

00:16:44.080 --> 00:16:47.560
The pigs' food is still here.

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:49.200
OK.

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:49.560
So she wants to call them in here.

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:51.120
She's going to call them to the house?

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:52.160
Yeah, to the house.

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:58.600
'And like all over Vanuatu, 'it's based around pigs, which are also used as currency.'

00:16:57.960 --> 00:17:04.200
So people live really closely with pigs, almost as if they are part of the family.

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:09.160
Particularly the pigs that start being reared for custom ceremonies.

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:17.640
'On Ambrym, pigs also play a fundamental role 'in the way society works.'

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:23.560
Let's just have a little piggy moment.

00:17:22.839 --> 00:17:25.680
Look how handsome you are.

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:32.160
'Pigs are used to change social status 'and their value depends on their tusks.'

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:37.320
So pigs are ranked according to the level by which their tusks are grown.

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:39.600
So the most valuable pig will have the tusk that's gone round two, sometimes even three times.

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:44.480
So it will go through the cheek, into the jaw and come back out again?

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:47.400
Yes.

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:56.480
This is the kind of pig that you would use if you were taking a rank in the ceremonial rituals of status alteration which men go through to gain rank in the sort of hierarchy of the village.

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:00.080
OK.

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:11.240
'Once its tusks have curled sufficiently, 'Janic is hoping to exchange his pig 'either for a bride, or for a new title 'so he can move up a rung in the island's social hierarchy.

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:16.480
'This grade system is called the Maghe.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:24.800
'In spite of embracing other aspects of the modern world, 'it's a custom that's still preserved.

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:26.920
'Public ceremonies take place once a year.'

00:18:38.000 --> 00:18:42.680
What we are seeing now is a tamoke dance.

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:47.720
So the tamoke dance is a spirit that dances through the village.

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:50.000
Is that the people that have got the masks?

00:18:49.280 --> 00:18:51.880
With the banana leaves.

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:52.360
With the banana leaves, yeah.

00:18:51.520 --> 00:19:01.200
They use it as an opening for the Magi, which is the system by which men will go through a series of different ranks.

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:06.280
And their status will get higher and higher as they do that.

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:11.200
'This is the grade taking ceremony.

00:19:10.280 --> 00:19:16.400
'The chief is going to assume one of 15 different grades.

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:28.440
'Each level brings men closer and closer to immortality 'and to the ancestors that reside in the volcano.'

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:39.480
You hear about some men who are so high ranking, they do mortuary ceremonies for them before they die because they have almost passed over into the upper realm.

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:44.000
'The chief is pelted with stones 'as a way to display his strength and agility.

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:59.560
'But to get his new title, 'he also has to hand over his most prized tusker pig 'and supply another normal pig for slaughter 'to show he can provide for his community.'

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:05.000
So they have these big special clubs that you can see there.

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:05.520
They should do it in one blow and then it's painless.

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:06.000
I can't watch that, I'm afraid.

00:20:10.440 --> 00:20:15.560
'It's not until the pig is killed that the exchange is complete.

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:23.600
'Ceremonies like this play a crucial role in safeguarding knowledge 'and tradition for future generations.

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:28.600
'And it's shown me a very different side to this island 'and its volcano.'

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:43.000
Marum is central to my whole journey on Ambrym, but it's really interesting having these two different approaches to understanding it.

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:55.200
One is the geological, knowing what those forces are that drive it, knowing and trying to discover what sort of state of activity it's in now.

00:20:54.560 --> 00:21:06.280
But equally important, I'm beginning to realise, is the custom and tradition that's also wrapped up around this volcano.

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:12.080
It's absolutely fundamental to the identity of this island and the people who created it.

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:30.160
We've already gathered important evidence from one of the subterranean vents, but there's still vital data Shane wants to collect before we set off for the volcano itself and descend into its heart.

00:21:30.920 --> 00:21:39.280
Something I'm in two minds about - both excited and frankly terrified.

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:45.920
That cave that we went to, I'm pretty sure that there will be hundreds of those along the coast.

00:21:44.960 --> 00:21:46.000
There must be.

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:51.720
There can't be an isolated one.

00:21:51.080 --> 00:22:03.120
While the rivers of molten rock that come with major eruptions are destructive and deadly, they're not the only danger Marum poses to the islanders.

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:15.760
Its permanently bubbling lava lake brings another equally lethal threat, in the form of its gas plume, so large, it's visible from space.

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:17.400
We're on our way to a village on the west of the island.

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:20.760
It's an area plagued by the deadly concoction of gases that pour out of the volcano.

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:36.920
So, here we are with these beautiful green hills in the foreground, all the jungles growing there, it's like the Garden of Eden, and then the hill beyond - there's no vegetation on those upper slopes.

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:37.200
It's not altitude controlled.

00:22:36.280 --> 00:22:47.120
It's controlled by the acidic gases, particularly, that are raining out from that cloud and constantly killing all the vegetation.

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:49.440
So the reason there's no vegetation up there is simply because of the gas.

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:52.120
It's killing all the trees.

00:22:51.240 --> 00:23:06.040
The people living in and around this area are constantly having to deal with this acid rain and acid gas situation.

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:13.160
Volcanic ash creates some of the most fertile soil in the world.

00:23:12.480 --> 00:23:20.720
But the toxic gases seep into the soil and into the water.

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:25.920
This village happens to be right on the axis of activity.

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:37.640
'We've come to Lolibulo, where in 2005, people were evacuated 'because of the huge amounts 'of toxic gas that was pumping out of Marum.'

00:23:36.760 --> 00:23:45.800
When it rained, and the rain then washes through the plume, and it takes with it sulphur, fluorine...

00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:58.360
'Fluorine is good for teeth, in small quantities, but too 'much causes severe tooth decay and debilitating brittle bone disease.

00:23:57.480 --> 00:24:02.880
'Shane regularly tests the water here.'

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:05.480
Fill that up.

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:11.640
Stick one of these tablets in each of the...

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:16.400
What do these do?

00:24:15.440 --> 00:24:22.800
We're going to change the chemistry of the water and if there's fluorine in here, it will react.

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:24.320
Crush that bottom.

00:24:26.920 --> 00:24:37.440
So, have there been, in the past, very high incidences of children in this village with evidence of brittle bones and bad teeth?

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:38.480
That kind of thing?

00:24:37.880 --> 00:24:48.080
In this village and the villages nearby, there was a really high incidents of chronic dental fluorosis, which is a strong indicator that there's bone issues as well.

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:48.600
Our first result's here.

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:49.520
OK.

00:24:49.200 --> 00:24:51.840
So, we've got a reading of...

00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:53.680
2.6.

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:55.040
2.6.

00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:56.080
The second one...

00:24:57.560 --> 00:25:01.320
2.72.

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:03.160
Wow!

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:05.520
Almost identical.

00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:09.440
What would normal levels be in clean or unadulterated rainwater?

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:10.560
Clean rainwater should have nothing.

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:11.200
Wow!

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:11.920
OK.

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:15.600
Should have more or less nothing.

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:18.200
OK.

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:18.720
I mean, is this health threatening?

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:18.880
This is...

00:25:17.960 --> 00:25:23.040
This is, in chronic terms, yes, so it's too high.

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:24.280
It's way too high.

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:26.000
More than twice as much as it should be.

00:25:27.360 --> 00:25:34.920
It's shocking to discover how contaminated the water in this village is.

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:53.640
While the people can reap the benefits of the fertile soil, the plume of gas they live beneath is more noxious than the air in any city.

00:25:57.640 --> 00:26:04.920
The team on the summit is almost ready for us, but the weather up there has been wildly fluctuating.

00:26:04.080 --> 00:26:12.840
Driving rain and strong winds would at the moment make any attempt to descend into the crater impossible.

00:26:11.920 --> 00:26:16.760
All we can do is hope that things change before we set off.

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:39.360
In the meantime, I want to go in search of some unusual wildlife that takes advantage of conditions on the island.

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:50.640
The megapode is a rare bird which incubates its eggs in the warm volcanic soils.

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:57.280
So, with my expert guide George, I'm on the hunt for eggs.

00:26:57.280 --> 00:27:02.040
So, what are the signs that we're looking for.

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:05.000
These birds are quite clever.

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:07.360
They usually come up last over the nest.

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:13.080
We can tell if there's lots of leaves and rotting wood and the ground is soft...

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:25.680
'Megapodes are endangered, so to protect them, 'it's taboo to collect their eggs, 'except at certain times of the year.'

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:31.520
It sort of...doesn't feel that warm, actually.

00:27:32.920 --> 00:27:36.560
It will get warmer.

00:27:35.600 --> 00:27:37.000
It will?

00:27:37.840 --> 00:27:39.160
Look at the size of it!

00:27:38.840 --> 00:27:46.000
Before we pull it out, let's take a temperature reading, cos I don't think it feels very warm.

00:27:49.080 --> 00:28:05.960
Which doesn't seem warm enough, but usually across the Pacific, for them to successfully incubate, people have found that the temperature is around 33 degrees, so I would say that's maybe a bit too cool, but do you sometimes find eggs that won't hatch?

00:28:06.240 --> 00:28:08.920
We find lots of eggs that don't hatch.

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:09.920
OK.

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:10.480
And they stink.

00:28:09.640 --> 00:28:11.040
Oh, really?

00:28:10.320 --> 00:28:12.440
Very smelly.

00:28:11.960 --> 00:28:13.040
Let me take it out now.

00:28:12.120 --> 00:28:13.240
Oh, my goodness!

00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:16.120
Look at that!

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:20.760
Look at the extraordinary shape and weight of this egg.

00:28:19.960 --> 00:28:21.200
It's really, really heavy.

00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:43.200
And if this egg were allowed to go through its full incubation period of about, of about 60 days, what would happen is that the chick very well fed on that enormous yolk, would use its feet to break out of the shell and then burrow up through the ground...

00:28:42.400 --> 00:28:51.400
And then it will emerge fully-feathered and within 24 hours will be able to be completely independent.

00:28:50.640 --> 00:28:54.000
Do you think there's going to be more?

00:28:53.120 --> 00:28:55.000
I think there should be more.

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:55.320
God!

00:28:54.400 --> 00:28:58.920
They go right in, don't they?

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:09.120
Maybe it's too far.

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:11.200
You're talking to a girl who likes a challenge.

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:11.800
Ha-ha!

00:29:11.240 --> 00:29:12.360
OK.

00:29:11.400 --> 00:29:17.400
So, you might have to drag me out here.

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:18.680
Ha-ha!

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:24.480
'The eggs are often buried two metres deep.

00:29:24.200 --> 00:29:27.560
'And these birds have done such a good job that our attempts to 'reach them make the entire bank unstable.'

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:28.960
It's really warm, George!

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:29.400
Argh!

00:29:32.280 --> 00:29:33.320
Whoa!

00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:33.680
Are you all right?

00:29:33.760 --> 00:29:34.840
Yes.

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:38.800
I'm fine!

00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:44.680
I don't know why they don't just go to the supermarket like everybody else!

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:48.280
Tell you what...

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:53.200
That's one of the difficulties of harvesting the eggs...

00:29:52.440 --> 00:29:55.000
Even though they're delicious...

00:29:54.160 --> 00:29:59.080
Even though they're delicious, it's hard to get them.

00:29:58.240 --> 00:30:01.240
We'll be lucky...

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:01.600
Lucky.

00:30:00.760 --> 00:30:02.920
God!

00:30:02.120 --> 00:30:04.000
I can't see a thing!

00:30:03.200 --> 00:30:09.040
'It probably serves me right for disturbing the nest.

00:30:09.920 --> 00:30:13.560
'I only hope the eggs are worth it.'

00:30:12.680 --> 00:30:14.680
Asbestos fingers.

00:30:13.720 --> 00:30:14.840
Also one thing about this egg.

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:24.080
It doesn't peel off nice and clean like a chicken egg.

00:30:23.240 --> 00:30:25.200
OK.

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:30.800
So, all in all, you nearly die when you collect them.

00:30:29.920 --> 00:30:31.120
When you cook them, they might be rotten.

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:31.840
They might be rotten.

00:30:30.920 --> 00:30:32.160
They don't peel easily.

00:30:31.240 --> 00:30:32.440
No.

00:30:31.560 --> 00:30:35.200
I hope they taste really good!

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:35.480
You'll soon find out.

00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:37.760
Just dip in like that?

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:38.000
Just dip in.

00:30:46.280 --> 00:30:48.440
That's delicious, isn't it?

00:30:49.920 --> 00:31:06.720
It's incredible to see how these extraordinary birds have adapted to their environment, and it's another reminder of how the volcano shapes life on every single part of this island.

00:31:07.080 --> 00:31:19.080
The time has finally come to head up to the summit and to meet Marum face to face.

00:31:28.360 --> 00:31:32.160
OK.

00:31:31.720 --> 00:31:33.480
Ready to roll?

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:34.120
Let's go.

00:31:36.080 --> 00:31:48.240
It's early morning and although the weather is fine down here, it's no indication of what it might be like at the top.

00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:50.520
Is that your water bottle?

00:31:49.720 --> 00:31:51.120
Straight up?

00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:51.520
No?

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:53.600
Off we go.

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:07.800
The first half of the trek takes us through dense jungle.

00:32:06.840 --> 00:32:19.120
It's hot and humid, but thanks to our local guides Robert and Janic, we get some inside knowledge into the rainforest's weird and wonderful natural resources.

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:24.640
Do you want to try some juice out of a vine?

00:32:23.760 --> 00:32:25.000
Yeah!

00:32:24.040 --> 00:32:25.560
Yeah, yeah.

00:32:26.680 --> 00:32:28.480
Isn't that incredible?

00:32:27.520 --> 00:32:29.080
Yeah.

00:32:28.920 --> 00:32:32.200
Tastes lovely as well, doesn't it?

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:34.440
That sort of slightly fresh, woody taste.

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:49.200
After four strenuous hours, we've climbed almost 1000 metres above sea level and the path is getting steeper.

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:52.520
You can see how narrow the ridge is.

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:54.000
It's just like a knife edge, isn't it?

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:54.640
Yeah, yeah, this is the right one!

00:32:53.960 --> 00:33:00.200
I tell you what, I'm very pleased it's as vegetated as this otherwise it would be very scary indeed!

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:14.960
Eventually, we catch our first glimpse of Marum in the distance.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:15.400
If you look through there, you can see Marum, not very well, but...

00:33:14.720 --> 00:33:19.120
Just that sort of dark patch through the trees?

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:21.640
Yeah, that's it.

00:33:20.840 --> 00:33:23.280
Wow.

00:33:22.360 --> 00:33:25.760
It's a welcome boost for the final leg of our journey.

00:33:24.800 --> 00:33:32.800
But there's an important ritual Janic tells us we have to carry out before we can go any further.

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:39.160
Before we go down to the ash plane each one of you has to carry their each own bamboo.

00:33:38.360 --> 00:33:43.400
So you go down and each one, you throw the bamboo to the ash plane before all of us go all about the ash plane.

00:33:42.960 --> 00:33:44.600
OK.

00:33:46.080 --> 00:33:47.240
I mean only....

00:33:46.280 --> 00:33:47.480
Ah, OK.

00:33:46.520 --> 00:33:52.320
Because you're an old hand you don't need to put one down?

00:33:51.520 --> 00:33:53.560
Yeah, yeah.

00:33:52.920 --> 00:33:58.240
I think you should, though.

00:34:00.680 --> 00:34:06.600
If it rains and there's a howling gale, I'll blame you!

00:34:05.960 --> 00:34:10.440
Yeah?

00:34:09.840 --> 00:34:11.000
This is where you have to throw it?

00:34:10.120 --> 00:34:12.800
Just throw the bamboo.

00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:16.760
OK, so from here?

00:34:15.880 --> 00:34:23.679
Throwing bamboo onto the ash plain is an ancient ritual to appease the volcano's spirits and keep us safe.

00:34:22.800 --> 00:34:27.280
It's a reminder that we have to treat Marum with great respect.

00:34:30.639 --> 00:34:35.760
Yeah, it's quite different here now.

00:34:37.639 --> 00:34:48.600
After the dense forest, our journey takes us across an epic moonscape into the volcano's outer crater - its caldera.

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:52.400
From here, we begin our final ascent.

00:34:52.080 --> 00:34:55.520
Lot of smoke coming out of the crater now, we can say.

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:56.080
Yeah.

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:56.760
You can just see that line, with the wind.

00:34:56.920 --> 00:34:58.040
Yeah, yeah.

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:11.320
OK, as far as ridges go... ..how extreme is this one?

00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:16.240
This is the last one.

00:35:15.480 --> 00:35:17.280
Damn it, thought you were going to say, "You're doing fine".

00:35:16.400 --> 00:35:19.240
You're doing fine!

00:35:24.800 --> 00:35:27.880
You're going to cause a landslide!

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:34.680
Oh, Robert, you and I are going to come up at a sedate and sensible pace.

00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:38.080
Oh, my God, this is quite scary!

00:35:38.080 --> 00:35:42.480
Eventually, after almost eight hours, it's the moment we've all been waiting for.

00:35:41.640 --> 00:35:43.240
Whoo!

00:35:42.440 --> 00:35:43.640
Yes!

00:35:42.680 --> 00:35:45.040
Oh, my God!

00:35:44.200 --> 00:35:45.440
Oh!

00:35:46.880 --> 00:35:48.120
Oh, my... !

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:51.720
Amazing, isn't it?

00:35:53.200 --> 00:35:58.360
It's just...

00:35:57.560 --> 00:35:59.680
I can't believe what a clear view we're getting of it.

00:35:58.720 --> 00:36:04.000
It's just kicking up onto the top of that ledge!

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:07.160
Yeah, yeah, that's amazing.

00:36:07.120 --> 00:36:14.960
There's something really kind of inexplicable about looking at a sight like that, because it's so unfamiliar.

00:36:14.040 --> 00:36:20.720
This isn't something that your kind of normal life computes.

00:36:19.880 --> 00:36:22.680
It's just this sort of astonishing energy.

00:36:22.040 --> 00:36:31.160
This kind of boiling, seething energy that looks like it just wants to kind of leap up the mountain.

00:36:30.640 --> 00:36:32.720
It looks to me, Shane, this is a precursor to it just going, "Woof!"

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:34.000
Oh, yeah.

00:36:33.320 --> 00:36:38.760
"Oh, yeah", he says as we stand on the edge!

00:36:38.320 --> 00:36:39.800
We're looking so far down.

00:36:38.920 --> 00:36:42.480
I mean, that's splashing up a long way.

00:36:41.720 --> 00:36:48.360
This is much, much higher than I've seen it for a long time.

00:36:47.920 --> 00:37:08.920
This is one of just five lava lakes in the whole world and Shane is immediately struck by how much bigger and more vigorous it is than when he was last here three years ago.

00:37:07.960 --> 00:37:09.760
To find out why, we need to get much closer.

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:16.680
Shane and Chris want to collect rock and gas samples from as near to the lake as possible.

00:37:16.960 --> 00:37:23.240
It's now all down to the weather.

00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:26.400
The weather up here for the last three days has been spectacularly bad.

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:39.600
The team who have been setting up camp and rigging everything for us have been on the sat phone and all they could hear was just pounding rain and howling, howling winds.

00:37:38.960 --> 00:37:45.720
So, to get up here in these relatively benign conditions feels incredibly lucky.

00:37:45.000 --> 00:37:48.200
How long it will last, nobody knows.

00:37:53.920 --> 00:37:58.080
Hi, welcome to mountain camp.

00:37:57.440 --> 00:38:04.240
Here to meet us is intrepid adventurer Geoff Mackley...

00:38:03.400 --> 00:38:06.440
Welcome to Fire Mountain!

00:38:05.560 --> 00:38:07.840
I need to hug you!

00:38:07.200 --> 00:38:11.200
..And my long-time friend, climbing expert Tim Fogg.

00:38:10.480 --> 00:38:13.160
Lovely walk.

00:38:12.240 --> 00:38:14.000
It's good, isn't it?

00:38:13.720 --> 00:38:15.400
Isn't it beautiful?

00:38:14.440 --> 00:38:15.560
Spectacular.

00:38:14.840 --> 00:38:15.960
Together, they'll help us collect the samples we need.

00:38:15.120 --> 00:38:18.520
Hi, Gareth, good to see you.

00:38:18.760 --> 00:38:20.160
You've got a dog in camp.

00:38:19.200 --> 00:38:25.800
That makes me feel I'm going to be able to cope with almost anything.

00:38:24.840 --> 00:38:26.400
Rusty!

00:38:30.760 --> 00:38:34.160
What you doing?

00:38:33.440 --> 00:38:49.040
If all goes well, tomorrow we'll be descending into this fiery cauldron with nothing between us and this blistering, 100-metre wide lake of lava.

00:38:48.560 --> 00:38:52.200
But with so much to do, Shane wastes no time.

00:38:51.360 --> 00:39:02.760
He immediately gets cracking with his thermal imaging camera to find out how hot and how dynamic the lava is.

00:39:02.320 --> 00:39:14.720
It's the most glorious night, almost no wind, and we're getting some of the clearest views of the crater that we've had since we've been up here.

00:39:15.920 --> 00:39:18.480
That's a fantastic image, isn't it?

00:39:19.840 --> 00:39:24.800
Look at the different temperatures across the thing.

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:28.680
We're measuring 1013, so it's pretty hot down there.

00:39:27.880 --> 00:39:29.200
Wow.

00:39:28.240 --> 00:39:30.200
It is.

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:36.800
So this temperature jumping around is measuring the highest point of the lake at this precise moment.

00:39:36.200 --> 00:39:37.800
The highest temperature of the lake at this precise moment.

00:39:39.240 --> 00:39:41.320
I mean, it does seem particularly furious tonight.

00:39:40.360 --> 00:39:50.920
Will this camera also be able to help you to establish the rhythm and frequency of these pulses of what appears to be new lava constantly cycling back up to the surface?

00:39:54.400 --> 00:40:13.440
I think it's going to be a really good indication of the size of the reservoir, because if you've got a small pot that's continually churning then obviously it will cool down - but we've got constant temperatures.

00:40:12.800 --> 00:40:16.840
Frequently over 1,000, isn't it?

00:40:17.600 --> 00:40:22.080
And as you say, at this distance, to measure radiant heat of that magnitude is extraordinary and terrifying, really.

00:40:21.520 --> 00:40:22.600
It's amazing, isn't it?

00:40:23.120 --> 00:40:25.600
It really does look furious tonight.

00:40:24.640 --> 00:40:26.360
It does, it looks like a real hell's cauldron down there.

00:40:25.480 --> 00:40:30.800
Yeah!

00:40:32.560 --> 00:40:40.720
The readings reveal an extraordinary 60% increase in the lava lake's heat and energy since Shane was last here.

00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:49.920
And this suggests that more magma is welling up from deep below, priming Marum to become much more active.

00:40:58.120 --> 00:41:06.440
It's a slope of, I'd guess around 50 degrees, that swaps from...

00:41:05.640 --> 00:41:10.280
At daybreak, climbing expert Tim gathers us all together for our first briefing.

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:13.080
And it's not good news.

00:41:12.200 --> 00:41:15.200
The weather's not great.

00:41:14.440 --> 00:41:28.680
The horrible thing about it, is when it rains, it's not normal rain, it's acid rain and it's quite surprising how it starts to sting your eyes and your skin starts to feel a bit funny.

00:41:29.200 --> 00:41:30.560
You can't just get off and go inside.

00:41:29.800 --> 00:41:33.760
You're stuck, you're in there for hours and hours.

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:37.960
Just staying down there and waiting out the rain, really, is a pretty horrible option.

00:41:41.800 --> 00:41:48.440
The awful weather means it's just not safe to descend today.

00:41:47.640 --> 00:41:53.880
It's a huge disappointment, and, with so little time, all we can do is hope it gets better.

00:41:59.160 --> 00:42:02.080
These really fine layers...

00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:13.240
But there's still plenty to we can do - so Shane and I are heading into the densest part of the gas plume to take some measurements.

00:42:12.360 --> 00:42:15.840
You need a clear sky above the gas.

00:42:16.720 --> 00:42:18.000
Do you think it's looking clear enough?

00:42:17.160 --> 00:42:21.360
Well, we're starting to get some good, blue sky.

00:42:20.480 --> 00:42:24.520
The plume is pretty thick but we'll have a go.

00:42:23.680 --> 00:42:24.800
OK.

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:27.840
Shall we use this rock here?

00:42:27.800 --> 00:42:30.560
Just in front of the ash, yeah.

00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:37.160
So the last time you were here in 2012, presumably you took gas measurements then.

00:42:36.200 --> 00:42:37.920
Yep.

00:42:37.080 --> 00:42:39.600
If they're higher than they were then, what will it tell you about the volcano now?

00:42:40.360 --> 00:42:57.360
Yeah, I think, if they're going up, it's telling us that there's perhaps either more material coming into the deeper magma system and that gas is bubbling through, or it could mean we're getting deeper magma that is on its way up and that would probably herald a new phase of greater activity.

00:43:05.240 --> 00:43:13.200
It's starting to creep up, basically just as the wind blows across.

00:43:13.120 --> 00:43:22.320
We're up to 400 now so I'm thinking we're going to get some pretty high values once we start to get into the centre of the plume.

00:43:24.280 --> 00:43:31.760
The increasing levels of gas show that Marum has become much more active.

00:43:30.960 --> 00:43:37.560
If the figures reach as high as they were a decade ago, the islanders may need to be evacuated once more.

00:43:36.760 --> 00:43:47.760
But to get the complete story, we need that good weather so we can descend into the crater itself.

00:43:49.840 --> 00:43:57.840
Later that night Chris and Shane spot a strange pink glow half a mile from the camp.

00:43:56.920 --> 00:43:58.160
That is really something.

00:43:57.280 --> 00:44:02.440
They suspect it could be a new lava lake.

00:44:01.480 --> 00:44:06.240
Look at it really, really pulsing.

00:44:05.600 --> 00:44:06.960
Yeah.

00:44:07.240 --> 00:44:21.480
It's partly obscured by gas but the thermal camera confirms their suspicions, revealing an entirely different lava lake, one that was last active just before the devastating gas plume in 2005.

00:44:23.480 --> 00:44:25.680
That's beautiful, isn't it?

00:44:24.720 --> 00:44:26.000
Yeah!

00:44:25.120 --> 00:44:34.480
It's a dramatic turn of events, all adding up to suggest something momentous is happening deep inside the volcano.

00:44:54.040 --> 00:45:01.520
It's our final morning and we all nervously wait for a break in the weather.

00:45:00.720 --> 00:45:03.160
No-one wants to leave without the vital data.

00:45:02.720 --> 00:45:26.280
For me now being here, to have this opportunity, if the weather is kind, to get down, even part of the way, down into the crater of an incredibly active volcano is sort of equally terrifying but absolutely irresistible.

00:45:25.400 --> 00:45:26.520
It's irresistible.

00:45:25.680 --> 00:45:31.640
I have got to get down there.

00:45:35.560 --> 00:45:42.080
At last the sun puts in an appearance, it looks just about safe enough to risk it.

00:45:47.760 --> 00:46:08.240
The idea of hanging over a boiling lava lake seems more than a little crazy, but the rock and gas samples from deep inside the crater are the final pieces of the puzzle Shane needs to fully understand what this volcano is doing.

00:46:07.560 --> 00:46:10.680
So are you ready to be a guinea-pig.

00:46:09.840 --> 00:46:11.040
I am.

00:46:10.080 --> 00:46:15.000
These are like dip sticks in that they're a glass tube.

00:46:14.520 --> 00:46:15.640
Yes.

00:46:14.680 --> 00:46:23.960
And then during your descent and return over that period the gases you encounter will react with the chemical on the foam in here.

00:46:23.760 --> 00:46:31.800
I'm going to put them facing downwards so we don't get ash in them.

00:46:31.040 --> 00:46:34.000
OK.

00:46:35.600 --> 00:46:40.600
When you get back we'll take them off and see how much gas you've been breathing.

00:46:44.840 --> 00:46:50.280
Right, where's Tim?

00:46:51.520 --> 00:46:54.960
You want to squeeze that lever.

00:46:54.280 --> 00:46:56.200
Yeah?

00:46:56.480 --> 00:47:00.440
'Once we're clipped in there's no turning back.'

00:46:59.640 --> 00:47:06.480
'We plan to abseil down this near vertical drop 'to a ledge above the boiling lake of lava.'

00:47:05.800 --> 00:47:08.040
This line pulls one, we're trying to fit two into it.

00:47:07.080 --> 00:47:08.600
Shall I come back down?

00:47:11.160 --> 00:47:24.520
'The tubes on my back should detect, the precise mix of gas coming off the lava lake.'

00:47:23.720 --> 00:47:25.640
Oh!

00:47:24.800 --> 00:47:26.640
Heart's racing.

00:47:25.760 --> 00:47:28.640
OK.

00:47:32.960 --> 00:47:38.400
Get it out of that groove.

00:47:45.880 --> 00:48:07.800
So, um, it's really hard to describe this feeling of descending to...well, certain death, obviously, if anything goes wrong but the exhilaration of doing something like this, it makes you feel very connected to the earth and how it works, if that makes any sort of sense at all.

00:48:10.880 --> 00:48:20.680
I have to confess that all I'm looking at, at the moment is my feet and Tim's feet.

00:48:25.440 --> 00:48:28.520
'The heat from the lake is intense.

00:48:27.920 --> 00:48:33.760
'And the noxious gases mean we can't spend long here 'so we have to move fast.

00:48:33.880 --> 00:48:39.080
'But the crumbling rocks, make it difficult to get a foothold.'

00:48:38.240 --> 00:48:40.000
Lean back, feet apart.

00:48:40.600 --> 00:48:42.120
Legs straight as you can.

00:48:41.240 --> 00:48:42.920
Oh, sorry!

00:48:42.040 --> 00:48:43.320
Oh, Tim, sorry!

00:48:42.400 --> 00:48:44.840
OK, OK, no problem.

00:48:47.040 --> 00:48:51.120
You can feel the heat now.

00:48:50.400 --> 00:48:58.200
I don't know whether it's just fear, sweat of fear and fear, or the heat of the lake itself.

00:48:59.320 --> 00:49:06.040
I can hear the sort of lava waves crashing.

00:49:09.120 --> 00:49:19.240
It really does feel like you're approaching some sort of extraordinarily angry sea.

00:49:18.280 --> 00:49:21.640
Gas is just one part of our quest.

00:49:21.160 --> 00:49:24.960
Shane also wants to collect rock samples from inside the crater.

00:49:24.040 --> 00:49:30.560
Just offer that to the next volcano god.

00:49:33.200 --> 00:49:42.320
Chris will analyse them to see if any of the newer lava has a similar composition to the lava from previous major eruptions.

00:49:42.840 --> 00:49:44.760
If so, it could mean Marum is heading for another big eruption.

00:49:45.200 --> 00:49:49.000
Come on!

00:49:48.240 --> 00:49:49.520
Are we there?

00:49:48.560 --> 00:49:51.000
Yes.

00:49:50.200 --> 00:49:52.600
Finally Tim and I reach the ledge we've been aiming for.

00:49:54.680 --> 00:50:04.960
The deafening roar of the boiling lava drowns out everything and the heat coming off the lake is almost overwhelming.

00:50:06.960 --> 00:50:11.560
What we'll do is to slowly adjust these knots so you can go forward.

00:50:10.760 --> 00:50:13.600
I don't want to go forward.

00:50:12.840 --> 00:50:14.400
I really don't.

00:50:13.680 --> 00:50:15.560
I really don't, I just...

00:50:14.680 --> 00:50:15.960
Not yet anyway.

00:50:15.000 --> 00:50:16.240
Let me...

00:50:15.440 --> 00:50:17.120
I've got you.

00:50:16.160 --> 00:50:20.240
I know but I don't want to pull you over the edge, Tim.

00:50:19.360 --> 00:50:22.400
It's just so loose, this rock.

00:50:21.600 --> 00:50:29.040
'I'm clinging on for what feels like dear life, 'but to get the best samples I have to get even closer to the edge.'

00:50:31.800 --> 00:50:34.800
'You go back to your left.'

00:50:36.240 --> 00:50:39.240
They don't want me right on the edge, do they?

00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:41.440
Right on the white rock?

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:43.560
Yes, that way a bit more.

00:50:43.160 --> 00:50:44.600
Tim, you've got to be kidding me.

00:50:47.440 --> 00:50:53.800
Centimetre by centimetre towards the white rock.

00:50:52.960 --> 00:50:54.080
Oh, wow!

00:50:53.160 --> 00:50:54.520
OK.

00:51:04.920 --> 00:51:19.200
The cloud is cleared, Tim has forced me to move sideways across this slope, but it was absolutely worth it.

00:51:20.000 --> 00:51:24.240
We are now getting the most astonishing views of the lava lake.

00:51:24.240 --> 00:51:28.360
Furious boiling lake of molten rock.

00:51:27.440 --> 00:51:39.560
Now that we're this much closer it really does look like it could just explode up at any moment.

00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:40.360
Oh, that was a huge one.

00:51:39.520 --> 00:51:49.160
It really looks like it's barely able to suppress itself.

00:51:48.200 --> 00:52:08.560
It's sort of like witnessing the beginning of the Earth... ..but it's the most astonishing feeling really, to be down the crater of a very active volcano, and these glass vials on my back that Shane put there will hopefully give him some sort of clues as how active this volcano really is at this very moment.

00:52:07.680 --> 00:52:12.560
The conditions are intense.

00:52:15.400 --> 00:52:27.400
The gas mask keeps out most of the noxious gases, but I can feel my throat burning and the heat is becoming unbearable.

00:52:27.760 --> 00:52:35.920
It's among the most amazing, terrifying and exhilarating two hours of my life.

00:52:35.000 --> 00:52:40.320
But I have to go while I've got the strength to get back to the top.

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:41.000
Slide yourself up.

00:52:46.040 --> 00:52:49.240
Whoo!

00:52:48.400 --> 00:52:50.360
Wow!

00:52:51.920 --> 00:52:56.160
It looked furious, like we shouldn't have been there.

00:52:55.280 --> 00:53:07.800
It was like a warning, and then as we just sat a bit calmly and actually everything around started to clear, it's so magnificent it kind of takes your breath away.

00:53:08.920 --> 00:53:25.080
And the lake sort of calmed, it was almost like, you know, "Well, OK, you can hang around for a bit," and then suddenly it was like this great burst of fury again, and really chucking lava out, you know, not just over the rim, but right up into the sky.

00:53:24.120 --> 00:53:26.200
And the coming up bit, you think, "Oh, it's going to be really easy.

00:53:25.240 --> 00:53:27.480
"I'll just lie back!"

00:53:26.720 --> 00:53:28.080
But it's not.

00:53:27.600 --> 00:53:32.880
I tell you what, my thighs are burning.

00:53:32.280 --> 00:53:35.440
But I'm desperate to know what's happened on the back?

00:53:34.520 --> 00:53:37.880
Are they still sitting there my little glass...?

00:53:37.720 --> 00:53:41.800
They're still there, they're still sitting there.

00:53:40.920 --> 00:53:43.800
Yes, and one of them's gone all yellow.

00:53:42.960 --> 00:53:44.120
Let's have a look.

00:53:43.160 --> 00:53:45.640
Looks like you've had a bit of noxious gas?

00:53:44.680 --> 00:53:46.440
Yes.

00:53:45.600 --> 00:53:48.120
This is SO2.

00:53:47.320 --> 00:53:49.200
That's sulphur dioxide.

00:53:48.480 --> 00:53:49.880
That's right.

00:53:49.320 --> 00:53:52.320
So you would expect a lot of that.

00:53:51.760 --> 00:53:56.800
Yes, because we've seen all that brown gas everywhere.

00:53:56.480 --> 00:53:59.840
Do you see that slight pink colour?

00:53:59.040 --> 00:54:00.120
Yes.

00:53:59.160 --> 00:54:00.600
You haven't had much of that.

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:00.960
What's that?

00:54:00.080 --> 00:54:01.320
That's HF.

00:54:00.560 --> 00:54:03.320
What's HF?

00:54:02.640 --> 00:54:03.880
Yeah, just as well.

00:54:03.040 --> 00:54:04.200
HF's pretty...

00:54:03.320 --> 00:54:04.480
Oh, it's the really horrible, acidic one.

00:54:03.560 --> 00:54:04.800
It's very, very poisonous.

00:54:04.120 --> 00:54:06.760
Is that the hydrogen fluoride?

00:54:05.800 --> 00:54:07.000
Exactly, yes.

00:54:06.040 --> 00:54:09.640
And presumably you're only able to get that reading because we were, I was, I should say!

00:54:08.760 --> 00:54:10.160
Because you went down there.

00:54:09.720 --> 00:54:10.920
Yes.

00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:12.840
I was physically down there, much, much closer to the lake.

00:54:11.880 --> 00:54:13.360
Closer, yeah.

00:54:12.640 --> 00:54:20.880
This is really quite spectacular.

00:54:23.120 --> 00:54:27.800
It's a huge relief that we're all back on solid ground.

00:54:27.120 --> 00:54:33.280
But there's no rest for Shane and Chris who head off to analyse their results.

00:54:32.440 --> 00:54:44.640
They need to compare the evidence we've found this time, to the data they already have from the large eruptions in 1989 and 2005.

00:54:47.080 --> 00:54:53.520
All I can do is wait to find out if we've uncovered Marum's secrets.

00:54:59.280 --> 00:55:04.320
Let's start with those gas results that you and I collected yesterday.

00:55:03.360 --> 00:55:08.960
We are getting 2,200 tonnes a day of sulphur dioxide.

00:55:08.960 --> 00:55:14.920
Now, in normal kind of conditions, say you were here in 2012, how did that compare?

00:55:14.360 --> 00:55:17.800
Yeah, so normal conditions like the last time for example, about 1,000.

00:55:16.920 --> 00:55:25.960
So it's over twice the amount of sulphur dioxide coming out of it now.

00:55:26.360 --> 00:55:39.280
The gas measurements Shane's taken across the island show the levels are rising towards those in 2005 when toxic gas emissions led to widespread evacuations.

00:55:38.480 --> 00:55:39.640
And the rock samples have their own tale to tell.

00:55:41.200 --> 00:55:54.080
Certainly the analysis that we did, suggested that the activity that's occurring today is similar to the 2005 or the 1989 eruptions.

00:55:57.320 --> 00:56:03.440
So, the composition of the lava seems much like it was during the last two big eruptions.

00:56:03.160 --> 00:56:08.520
And then there's the unexpected discovery of the new lava lake.

00:56:07.800 --> 00:56:09.640
I was blown away.

00:56:08.680 --> 00:56:10.240
Yeah, it was an amazing experience.

00:56:09.600 --> 00:56:21.880
But surely, surely that, combined with the data that you've got, must indicate something, well, quite exciting with regards to the whole island's volcanic activity, doesn't it?

00:56:25.200 --> 00:56:28.760
Something's certainly on the go.

00:56:28.040 --> 00:56:36.080
You know, there's definitely, we're definitely double background level, so we're not just sitting there at normal level.

00:56:35.320 --> 00:56:36.680
Things are going up.

00:56:35.800 --> 00:56:38.960
Volcanoes are very unpredictable.

00:56:38.160 --> 00:56:47.120
We haven't had anything a big lava flow like 1989 for a while, so I would have said, yes, we're overdue for something like that.

00:56:54.280 --> 00:57:04.480
The evidence we've collected suggests something is stirring deep inside the heart of this volcano, bringing the threat of a big eruption ever closer.

00:57:03.640 --> 00:57:12.480
And our discoveries mean that Shane now feels there's enough evidence to increase Marum's level of risk on the international register.

00:57:11.600 --> 00:57:22.320
It's an early warning for the islanders to prepare should they have to evacuate.

00:57:21.480 --> 00:57:33.040
This has been the most astonishing journey around the volcanoes of Vanuatu.

00:57:32.240 --> 00:57:50.480
And to sit here now, looking down at Marum's magnificent lava lake boiling away, and to understand just a little bit more about its personality and to discover that we are potentially on the brink of a time of really heightened activity is tremendously exciting.

00:58:06.160 --> 00:58:12.240
'It's time to bid farewell to these breathtaking islands 'and their spectacular volcanoes.'

00:58:11.480 --> 00:58:13.800
Oh, my goodness!

00:58:13.240 --> 00:58:14.800
Oh, it's so beautiful!

00:58:14.720 --> 00:58:21.400
'It is without question, 'an experience I'll never forget.'

00:58:20.600 --> 00:58:25.920
Oh, my God!

00:58:25.000 --> 00:58:39.560
'And it's reassuring to know that the data we've collected and the knowledge we've gained will help to keep the people living here safe from harm.

00:59:13.320 --> 00:59:14.360
Get ready for the blind auditions!

00:59:21.000 --> 00:59:25.480
Yeah, I'm trained at this.

00:59:29.160 --> 00:59:30.280
Right, then.

00:59:31.960 --> 00:59:38.120
Yeah!

00:59:37.160 --> 00:59:38.680
My grandad was called Tom Jones.

00:59:39.200 --> 00:59:41.600
Are you sure it wasn't me?

00:59:40.640 --> 00:59:42.040
I knew it!

00:59:41.200 --> 00:59:42.840
You need to be on my team.

00:59:48.200 --> 00:59:49.320
That's classic.
